import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Start Here: Your First Bitcoin | The Crypto Realm" description="A beginner's guide to buying Bitcoin, moving it into your own wallet, and avoiding the mistakes that cost people the most.">
  <section class="container page-title">
    <div class="eyebrow">START HERE</div>
    <h1>Your First Bitcoin: A Beginner’s Guide</h1>
    <p class="subtle">How to buy Bitcoin, move it into a wallet that only you control, and avoid the mistakes that cost people the most. It takes an afternoon, and you can start with a small amount.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="container section">
    <div class="disclosure">Some links on this page are referral or affiliate links. If you sign up or buy through them, I may earn a reward or commission at no extra cost to you. Exchanges aren’t available in every country, so check what’s offered where you live. Crypto is risky and prices can fall sharply. This guide is general information, not financial advice. Please read the <a href="/disclaimer">disclaimer</a>.</div>

    <ol class="steps">
      <li><span>1</span><h3>Learn the basics</h3><p>Five terms are all you need.</p></li>
      <li><span>2</span><h3>Buy on an exchange</h3><p>The easiest way in.</p></li>
      <li><span>3</span><h3>Get your own wallet</h3><p>So you hold the keys.</p></li>
      <li><span>4</span><h3>Move your coins</h3><p>Start with a small test.</p></li>
      <li><span>5</span><h3>Stay safe</h3><p>Avoid the common scams.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="container section" id="basics">
    <div class="section-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">STEP 1</div><h2>The basics in plain English</h2></div></div>
    <div class="terms">
      <div class="card term"><h3>Bitcoin</h3><p>A digital money that runs on a network of computers, with no company or government in charge. Only 21 million will ever exist.</p></div>
      <div class="card term"><h3>Exchange</h3><p>A company where you can buy and sell crypto with regular money, like a currency counter. It usually asks you to verify your identity.</p></div>
      <div class="card term"><h3>Wallet</h3><p>The tool you use to send and receive crypto. Your coins live on the blockchain, and the wallet holds the keys that control them.</p></div>
      <div class="card term"><h3>Recovery phrase</h3><p>A list of 12 or 24 words that can rebuild your wallet. Whoever has it controls your coins. No one can reset it for you.</p></div>
      <div class="card term"><h3>Self-custody</h3><p>Holding your own keys, so no company can freeze your coins or lose them. It also means you are responsible for keeping them safe.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container section" id="buy">
    <div class="section-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">STEP 2</div><h2>Buy on an exchange</h2></div></div>
    <div class="article-body">
      <p>For most people, an exchange is the easiest place to start. You create an account, verify your identity, link a bank account or card, and buy. Regulated exchanges are required to check who you are, so expect to upload a photo ID.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Start small.</strong> You can buy a fraction of a bitcoin, so you don’t need much to learn how it works.</li>
        <li><strong>Check the fees.</strong> They differ between exchanges and payment methods, and they change, so look at the total cost before you confirm.</li>
        <li><strong>Turn on two-factor login</strong> using an authenticator app, not text messages.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Here are the exchanges I use or have listed. You can compare them all on the <a href="/trade/">Trade page</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="cta-row">
      <a class="btn btn-outline" href="https://coinbase.com/join/TR4QRX8?src=android-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener sponsored">COINBASE ↗</a>
      <a class="btn btn-outline" href="https://crypto.com/app/rqfyfc3k2w" target="_blank" rel="noopener sponsored">CRYPTO.COM ↗</a>
      <a class="btn btn-outline" href="https://invite.kraken.com/JDNW/z1z9jie3" target="_blank" rel="noopener sponsored">KRAKEN ↗</a>
    </p>
    <div class="card warn"><h3>An exchange is not your wallet</h3><p>When your coins sit on an exchange, the exchange holds the keys. If it freezes your account or fails, you can be locked out. In November 2022 the exchange FTX collapsed and customers could not withdraw their funds. That is why the next step matters.</p></div>
  </section>

  <section class="container section" id="wallet">
    <div class="section-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">STEP 3</div><h2>Get your own wallet</h2></div></div>
    <div class="article-body">
      <p>A hardware wallet is a small device that keeps your keys offline, away from viruses and hackers. Your computer or phone only sees transactions you approve on the device itself. The Ledger Nano X is the one I use.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Buy only from the official site or a verified seller.</strong> Never from a stranger or a used listing.</li>
        <li><strong>Set it up yourself.</strong> If a device arrives with a recovery phrase already written down, or already set up, don’t use it.</li>
        <li><strong>Write your recovery phrase on paper</strong> and keep it somewhere safe. Never photograph it, type it into a phone or computer, or store it in the cloud.</li>
        <li><strong>Pick your own PIN.</strong></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <p class="cta-row"><a class="btn btn-primary" href="https://shop.ledger.com/pages/ledger-nano-x?r=0623129b4c5b" target="_blank" rel="noopener sponsored">GET THE LEDGER NANO X ↗</a></p>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="compare">
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>On an exchange</th><th>In your own wallet</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th>Who holds the keys</th><td>The exchange</td><td>You</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Can a company freeze it?</th><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
          <tr><th>If you forget your password</th><td>Usually recoverable through the company</td><td>Only with your recovery phrase</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Getting started</th><td>Easiest</td><td>Takes an afternoon to learn</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Best for</th><td>Buying and small amounts</td><td>Holding for the long term</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container section" id="move">
    <div class="section-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">STEP 4</div><h2>Move your coins</h2></div></div>
    <div class="article-body">
      <ol>
        <li>In your wallet app, choose <strong>Receive</strong> for Bitcoin and check the address on the device’s own screen.</li>
        <li>On the exchange, choose <strong>Withdraw</strong> or <strong>Send</strong> and paste the address. Compare the first and last few characters to the address on your device, because some malware swaps copied addresses.</li>
        <li><strong>Send a small test amount first.</strong> Wait until it arrives, then send the rest.</li>
        <li>Expect a network fee, and expect transfers to take some minutes to confirm.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Bitcoin transfers can’t be reversed. A wrong address means lost coins, which is why the test transfer is worth the few extra minutes.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container section" id="safe">
    <div class="section-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">STEP 5</div><h2>Stay safe</h2></div></div>
    <div class="safe-grid">
      <div class="card safe"><h3>Never share your recovery phrase</h3><p>No real support person, exchange or wallet company will ever ask for it. Anyone who does is a scammer.</p></div>
      <div class="card safe"><h3>Distrust messages about your wallet</h3><p>Emails and texts saying your wallet is “deactivated” or needs an “update” are a classic scam. Go to the official site yourself, using a bookmark.</p></div>
      <div class="card safe"><h3>Real example: Ledger, 2020</h3><p>Hackers leaked the contact details of about 272,000 Ledger customers, which set off waves of phishing and threats. Ledger said the devices and funds were not affected. It shows why you should doubt any message that claims to be from your wallet company.</p></div>
      <div class="card safe"><h3>Watch for fake giveaways and fake support</h3><p>“Send 1 and get 2 back,” surprise investment advisors, and strangers offering to help you recover coins are scams.</p></div>
      <div class="card safe"><h3>Only risk what you can afford to lose</h3><p>Bitcoin’s price can swing a lot in a single day. Don’t borrow to buy, and don’t use leverage while you’re learning.</p></div>
      <div class="card safe"><h3>Keep records for taxes</h3><p>In many countries, selling or spending crypto is a taxable event. Save your transaction history and check your local rules.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container section" id="faq">
    <div class="section-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">QUESTIONS</div><h2>Quick answers</h2></div></div>
    <div class="article-body">
      <h3>Is Bitcoin anonymous?</h3>
      <p>No. It is pseudonymous. Every transaction is public on the blockchain, and regulated exchanges link your identity to the coins you buy.</p>
      <h3>Can I lose my Bitcoin?</h3>
      <p>Yes. If you lose your recovery phrase and your device, nobody can recover your coins for you. That’s the price of having full control.</p>
      <h3>What about NFTs?</h3>
      <p>NFTs are digital certificates of ownership. They’re a separate topic, covered on our <a href="/nfts/">NFTs page</a>.</p>
      <h3>Where should I go next?</h3>
      <p>Read our <a href="/articles/">latest articles</a>, watch the <a href="/#videos">videos</a>, or browse the <a href="/trade/">Trade page</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container section">
    <p class="foot-note">This guide is general information, not financial, tax or legal advice. Crypto can lose value quickly and some of it can’t be recovered. Facts about the 2020 Ledger breach are from Ledger’s own statements and press reports.</p>
  </section>
</Base>

